---
import Deck from '../layouts/Deck.astro';
const profileCode = `<span class="kw">export type</span> ModelCapabilityProfile = {
  structuredPlanning: <span class="kw">boolean</span>;
  reasoning: ReasoningConfig;
};

<span class="kw">export function</span> getModelCapabilityProfile(model: <span class="kw">string</span>) {
  <span class="kw">if</span> (model.startsWith(<span class="kw">'meta/muse-spark-1.3'</span>)) {
    <span class="kw">return</span> { structuredPlanning: <span class="kw">false</span> };
  }
  <span class="kw">return</span> {
    structuredPlanning: <span class="kw">true</span>,
    reasoning: { effort: <span class="kw">'none'</span> },
  };
}`;
const providerCode = `{
  <span class="cm">"model"</span>: <span class="kw">"meta/muse-spark-1.3"</span>,
  <span class="cm">"provider"</span>: <span class="kw">"openrouter"</span>,
  <span class="cm">"structuredPlanning"</span>: <span class="kw">false</span>,
  <span class="cm">"toolSchemaVersion"</span>: <span class="kw">"capability-first-v2"</span>,
  <span class="cm">"failureCategory"</span>: <span class="kw">"provider-compatibility"</span>
}`;
---
<Deck title="Models Fail Differently" brand="MODEL CHOICE" series="EPISODE 05 — FAILURE SHAPES">
<deck-cue time="COVER" title="The title">Open on the title. There is no single best tool-calling model — there are models that fail in different ways.</deck-cue>
<deck-cue time="STEP 1 · THE SNAPSHOT" title="No single best model">Open on the results table: the same hard suite, five models, five failure shapes. These are fixture-specific engineering measurements, not a leaderboard.</deck-cue>
<deck-cue time="STEP 2 · THE RESULTS" title="Five models, one suite">One hard suite, five models, five failure shapes. Score is not rank — the cheapest model wins on this workload and the most expensive comes last.</deck-cue>
<deck-cue time="STEP 3 · THE SHAPE" title="A score hides the shape">Two models both score 5/10 and need opposite fixes: one picks irrelevant tools but keeps every id, the other picks the right tools and stops at confirmation because the harness never approved.</deck-cue>
<deck-cue time="STEP 4 · THE PROFILE" title="Profile in the system">Put the model profile in the system, not scattered through router branches. A model that can't emit the structured plan is isolated behind a compatibility profile.</deck-cue>
<deck-cue time="STEP 5 · THE DIMENSIONS" title="Product dimensions">Stop scoring one number. Score tool selection, argument accuracy, dependency accuracy, confirmation compliance, grounding, provider errors, unauthorized calls, latency, tokens, cost.</deck-cue>
<deck-cue time="STEP 6 · THE PROVIDER" title="Provider is the system">The model name is not the runtime. Provider defaults, schemas, reasoning controls, serialization, timeouts and retries shape the result. Record them.</deck-cue>
<deck-cue time="STEP 7 · THE POLICY" title="Choose by workload">The useful question is not which model wins. It is which model meets the safety and quality threshold for this workload within its budget.</deck-cue>
<deck-cue time="STEP 8 · CLOSE" title="Product policy">Routing is product policy. The strongest model is the one that meets the contract consistently enough to keep the user in control.</deck-cue>

<deck-scene id="s0" title="EPISODE 05 — FAILURE SHAPES" layout="full" cover>
<h1 class="rise">Models Fail Differently</h1>
<p class="lede rise">There is no single best tool-calling model — only models that fail in different ways.</p>
</deck-scene>

<deck-scene id="s1" title="THE SNAPSHOT" layout="full">
<h2 class="rise">Five models. <em class="serif">One hard suite.</em></h2>
<p class="lede rise">Same contract, same prompt, same fixtures. These are engineering measurements, not a general leaderboard.</p></deck-scene>

<deck-scene id="s2" title="THE RESULTS" layout="split"><div>
<h2 class="rise">Score is not <em class="serif">rank.</em></h2>
<p class="lede rise">The cheapest model wins here; the most expensive comes last — on this workload. Another workload moves the order.</p></div>
<div class="diagram rise"><div class="dlabel"><span>DIRECT RAW-TOOL HARD SUITE</span><span>FIXTURE-SPECIFIC</span></div>
<deck-metrics id="results">
<deck-row head><span>MODEL</span><span class="num">SCORE</span><span class="num">COST</span><span class="num">LATENCY</span></deck-row>
<deck-row><b>GPT-4o mini</b><span class="num">7/10</span><span class="num">$0.00086</span><span class="num">27.0s</span></deck-row>
<deck-row><b>GPT-4.1</b><span class="num">5/10</span><span class="num">$0.01239</span><span class="num">29.7s</span></deck-row>
<deck-row><b>Muse Spark 1.3</b><span class="num">3/10</span><span class="num">$0.03165</span><span class="num">102.1s</span></deck-row>
<deck-row><b>Claude Sonnet 4.5</b><span class="num">5/10</span><span class="num">$0.05949</span><span class="num">75.7s</span></deck-row>
<deck-row><b>GPT-5 mini</b><span class="num">5/10</span><span class="num">$0.01340</span><span class="num">113.7s</span></deck-row>
</deck-metrics></div></deck-scene>

<deck-scene id="s3" title="THE SHAPE" layout="full">
<h2 class="rise">A score hides <em class="serif">the failure shape.</em></h2>
<p class="lede rise">Three models score 5/10. They do not have the same problem, or the same fix.</p>
<button class="replay rise" data-replay="shape"><deck-icon name="rotate-ccw"></deck-icon> REPLAY THE SHAPES</button>
<deck-compare class="rise" style="margin-top:26px" id="shape">
<deck-col><h3>5/10 — WRONG TOOLS</h3><p>Chooses irrelevant tools.</p><p>Preserves every id.</p><p>Fix: selection and routing.</p></deck-col>
<deck-col><h3>5/10 — RIGHT TOOLS</h3><p>Correct tools, correct order.</p><p>Stops at confirmation — never approved.</p><p>Fix: the harness, not the model.</p></deck-col>
</deck-compare></deck-scene>

<deck-scene id="s4" title="THE PROFILE" layout="split"><div>
<h2 class="rise">The profile belongs<br><em class="serif">in the system.</em></h2>
<p class="lede rise">A model that cannot emit the requested structured plan is isolated behind one compatibility profile — not compensated for with provider-name checks in every branch.</p></div>
<div class="diagram rise"><div class="dlabel"><span>MODEL CAPABILITY PROFILE</span><span>ONE PLACE</span></div>
<deck-code id="profileCode" set:html={profileCode}></deck-code>
<p class="note">The benchmark can then separate weak planning from a provider rejecting structured output.</p></div></deck-scene>

<deck-scene id="s5" title="THE DIMENSIONS" layout="split"><div>
<h2 class="rise">Measure product<br><em class="serif">dimensions.</em></h2>
<p class="lede rise">One model can be right for cheap lookups; another is worth reserving for cross-domain planning. Only the dimensions say which.</p></div>
<div class="diagram rise"><div class="dlabel"><span>THE PROFILE OF A RUN</span><span>NOT ONE NUMBER</span></div>
<deck-grid id="dims" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr">
<deck-cell><b>Tool selection</b><span>the right tool</span></deck-cell>
<deck-cell><b>Argument accuracy</b><span>the facts survive</span></deck-cell>
<deck-cell><b>Dependency accuracy</b><span>prerequisites hold</span></deck-cell>
<deck-cell><b>Confirmation compliance</b><span>the boundary holds</span></deck-cell>
<deck-cell><b>Grounded answers</b><span>no invented fields</span></deck-cell>
<deck-cell><b>Unauthorized calls</b><span>must stay zero</span></deck-cell></deck-grid></div></deck-scene>

<deck-scene id="s6" title="THE PROVIDER" layout="split"><div>
<h2 class="rise">Provider behavior is<br><em class="serif">part of the system.</em></h2>
<p class="lede rise">Provider defaults, supported schemas, reasoning controls, serialization, timeouts and retries shape the observed result. Record them with the run.</p></div>
<div class="diagram rise"><div class="dlabel"><span>RUN METADATA</span><span>NOT THE MODEL'S FAULT</span></div>
<deck-code id="providerCode" set:html={providerCode}></deck-code>
<p class="note">Without this, someone rewrites the prompt to compensate for a transport incompatibility.</p></div></deck-scene>

<deck-scene id="s7" title="THE POLICY" layout="split"><div>
<h2 class="rise">Choose models<br>by <em class="serif">workload.</em></h2>
<p class="lede rise">No unauthorized writes, correct prerequisites, grounded answers — then latency, then cost. The order is explicit, and it moves with the surface.</p></div>
<div class="diagram rise"><div class="dlabel"><span>EXPECTED VALUE</span><span>PRODUCT POLICY</span></div>
<deck-code id="policyCode">const expectedValue =
  groundedAnswerRate * taskValue
  - costUsd
  - (providerErrorRate + unauthorizedCallRate)
    * safetyPenalty
  - latencyMs * latencyPenalty;</deck-code>
<deck-verdict><deck-case><b>WITHIN BUDGET</b>Meets the threshold for this workload.</deck-case><deck-case id="policyStop" state="refuse"><b>UNAUTHORIZED WRITE</b>The one term that never trades for cost.</deck-case></deck-verdict></div></deck-scene>

<deck-scene id="s8" title="CLOSE" layout="full">
<div class="finish rise">Not “which model wins.”<br><em class="serif">Which model keeps the user in control?</em></div>
<p class="lede rise" style="margin-top:28px">Shape, profiles, dimensions, provider, policy — routing as product policy. <deck-mark inline></deck-mark></p>
<deck-flow class="rise" style="margin-top:34px"><deck-step>NEXT VIDEO</deck-step><deck-step hot>THE PERSONAL AGENT IS A TYPED CAPABILITY SYSTEM</deck-step></deck-flow></deck-scene>
</Deck>

<script is:inline slot="script">
const { seq, pulse, register } = window.deckBuild;
register({
  shape: () => seq('#shape .col'),
  profiles: () => pulse('profileCode'),
  dimensions: () => seq('#dims .block'),
  provider: () => pulse('providerCode'),
  policy: () => { pulse('policyCode'); pulse('policyStop'); }
});
</script>
